From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Roman Leventov <leventov.ru@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 11:25:34 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] wasm: optimize static execution and native value
 boundaries

Expose a native UTF-8 string writer to Static Hermes extern calls and
allow Hermes string values as native arguments. Snapshot own enumerable
entries without JavaScript pair-array allocation, preserving getter and
proxy order.

Construct decoded objects using bounded runtime-local shape templates and
dense arrays through native slots. Keep templates rooted and independent
of document values; use general own-property definitions for indexed or
duplicate keys and mutable dictionary shapes. Add native descriptor
snapshots that compare data, accessors, symbols, prototypes and flags
without constructing descriptor objects or invoking accessors.

Emit compiled regex literal bytecode in Static Hermes C output while
creating fresh RegExp objects and retaining named-group initialization.
Add selective static recognition of original Math methods, excluding
random, and lower numeric imul to unsigned modulo-32-bit multiplication.
Other operands retain JavaScript coercion through the builtin call.

Validate object isolation and GC roots, descriptor mutations and SameValue
semantics, regex identity and state, numeric conversions, preserved host
replacements, and emitted native helper calls.

Resolve native object layouts from borrowed key bytes, compare collisions by
key content, and evict individual least-recently-used layouts. Build immutable
classes directly and decode values through callbacks into initialized, rooted
object and array storage. Retain explicit fallbacks for wide, indexed and
duplicate-key objects and expose construction counters without allocation.

Return the original primitive for full-range string slices and scan unchanged
ASCII case conversions before allocating temporary UTF-16 storage. Cache string
prototype data-property slots in Static Hermes while preserving replacements,
accessor receivers and prototype mutations.
